<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isle of Wight</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Vectis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Isle of Wight is an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as Vectis. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> records that it was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a>, and it was conquered by Caedwalla of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> in 686. Charles I was imprisoned in its Carisbrooke Castle in 1647–1648, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> died at her island residence, Osborne House, in 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isle-of-wight.jpg" alt="Isle of Wight" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA/Chris Hadfield · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IsleOfWightFromTheISS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Isle of Wight is separated from the mainland by the Solent strait, which lies between 2 and 5 miles wide, and is otherwise surrounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a>. It covers about 380 square kilometres, making it the largest island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and has 57 miles of coastline. A large monocline tilts the chalk beds to almost 90 degrees at the Needles. The climate is milder and sunnier than most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s, and the island is one of the few places in England where the European red squirrel still flourishes, since no grey squirrels live there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> occupation is shown by bell beaker pots, flint tools and the Arreton hoard of bronze, and by barrows on the chalk downland, such as the cemetery of Five Barrows near Brook. Julius Caesar reported that the Belgae took the island in about 85 BCE, and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suetonius">Suetonius</a> says that Vespasian captured it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built no towns there, but the remains of at least seven villas show a prosperous agriculture, with exports of hides, enslaved people, hunting dogs, grain, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and metals.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> of 1066 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> gave the island to his kinsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-fitzosbern">William FitzOsbern</a>, and Carisbrooke Priory and Carisbrooke Castle were founded. Allegiance was sworn to FitzOsbern rather than to the king. Henry I granted the lordship to the de Redvers family after he succeeded in 1100. John Keats worked on Endymion at Carisbrooke and Shanklin in 1817. In the mid-1840s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> blight was first found in the United Kingdom on the island. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> it was bombed often and hosted the RAF radar station at Ventnor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island is a ceremonial county and a unitary authority, with Newport as its administrative centre and Ryde, in the north-east, as its largest town. Until 1 April 1890, when the Isle of Wight County Council was formed, it was administered as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hampshire">Hampshire</a>, and it became a full ceremonial county in 1974. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a> built Osborne House at East Cowes as her summer residence and final home, and the poets Tennyson and Swinburne lived on the island. It has a tradition of boat-building, and Cowes hosts the annual Cowes Week. Tourism is its largest industry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isle of Wight” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Cædwalla (r. 685–688) the West Saxons conquered the <b>Isle of Wight</b> and Sussex and briefly held Kent, and Ine and his successors pushed into the British kingdom of Dumnonia, taking Devon in the late seventh and eighth centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Victoria died on the <b>Isle of Wight</b> aged 81 after reigning for 63 years, then the longest reign in British history.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-22">22 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Bede reports that the <b>Isle of Wight</b>, like Kent and part of Hampshire, was settled by Jutes.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-isle-of-wight">Kingdom of the Isle of Wight</a></li></ul></section>
